    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fa98f980_dff3569d",
        "filename": "keystone/contrib/federation/idp.py",
        "patchSetId": 19
      },
      "lineNbr": 478,
      "author": {
        "id": 6482
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-09-02T06:34:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I say remove the get_cert() function. I think sigver has sufficient error handling",
      "parentUuid": "fa98f980_9e7c7722",
      "revId": "e08f8769e3c3cb705e2642857065b10131d28a76",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fa98f980_703b6938",
        "filename": "keystone/contrib/federation/idp.py",
        "patchSetId": 19
      },
      "lineNbr": 478,
      "author": {
        "id": 8978
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-09-02T14:08:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Actually when I looked again in the sigver.read_cert_from_file() body (https://github.com/rohe/pysaml2/blob/master/src/saml2/sigver.py#L656) I noticed few things and I am not so sure sigver.read_cert_from_file() is sufficient (and even should be used): \n\n1) In case of problems it will raise standard IOError exception withoug logging it anywhere. I think we should log that something bad happened (or the cert file path is simply misconfigured) -\u003e I think we should wrap sigver.read_cert_file() somehow.\n\n2) a sigver.CertificateError is raised in case simple checks are not True. Do we really want to expose library specific exceptions to the Keystone ecosystem? I feel we should not really raise anything else that standard Python exceptions (IOError, ValueError, IndexError) and those listed in keystone.exception.py. That\u0027s why I proposed catching sigver.CertificateError and re raising as IOError.\n\n3) Inside sigver.read_cert_from_file() i can see the file is opened, but never closed. Looks like a serious bug inside the function? \n\nTherefore, for now on I will change to get_cert() but I expect some background whether wheher I should change to:\n\na) own implementation of the read_cert_from_file() (which  would be a fixed version of its predecessor)\nb) use simply sigver.read_cert_from_file() \nc) use get_cert() which somehow wraps sigver.read_cert_from_file()\n\nI personally vote for a).",
      "parentUuid": "fa98f980_dff3569d",
      "revId": "e08f8769e3c3cb705e2642857065b10131d28a76",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "fa98f980_9a68e388",
        "filename": "keystone/contrib/federation/idp.py",
        "patchSetId": 19
      },
      "lineNbr": 478,
      "author": {
        "id": 4
      },
      "writtenOn": "2014-09-02T16:40:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "1) it shouldn\u0027t log anything for us, but it\u0027s reasonable for us to wrap our call to it with a catch to log failures. so, I agree.\n\n2) it seems reasonable to re-raise these as an UnexpectedError with a custom message.\n\n3) read_cert_from_file() looks like it\u0027s going to leak file descriptors. that bug should be filed and addressed upstream. I\u0027m fine with (A) if that\u0027s viable, else file a bug against keystone that we\u0027re going to leak descriptors when we use that method, and target it to juno-3 / RC1. hopefully we\u0027ll be able to see an upstream release to fix that issue soon?",
      "parentUuid": "fa98f980_703b6938",
      "revId": "e08f8769e3c3cb705e2642857065b10131d28a76",
      "serverId": "4a232e18-c5a9-48ee-94c0-e04e7cca6543",
      "unresolved": false
    },
